}# Native `.7z` draft ‚Äî bounded filesystem exchange

This research draft remains in ignored work. It is not connected to the running Archive app. The earlier 42-test Zstandard slice and its receipt remain intact. Neither the current app nor the user‚Äôs browser was changed here.

`sevenzip.mjs` reads and writes genuine `.7z` containers. It supports one simple coder per folder: Copy (`00`), standard raw DEFLATE (`040108`), and LZMA2 (`21`) containing **only uncompressed chunks**. DEFLATE is the draft writer‚Äôs default and genuinely compresses data. LZMA2 stored chunks provide format exchange, not compression. Writers can use one folder per nonempty file or one solid folder; readers recover solid substreams. Plain headers and one level of encoded headers using these supported coders are accepted. The draft encoded-header writer uses DEFLATE.

The latest retained run passes **23/23 tests, zero skips**. The authenticated complete Windows x64 **7-Zip 26.04** executable tests and extracts all six candidate method/solid combinations, the DEFLATE encoded header, and a 129-entry partial-timestamp bitmap archive. Candidate reads original Copy and Deflate archives with solid mode on/off and header compression disabled. Nine further original archives exercise repeated alignment fields, Unicode names and creation/access/modified-time metadata. Names and types, SHA-256 content, and defined modification times agree. Test-produced original and candidate archives, extracted files and exact fixture hashes remain in `run-1791825897967/`. Source, executable, package, fixture and implementation hashes are in `draft-validation.json`.

Run from this folder using Node 24.16.0:

```powershell
node --test --test-concurrency=1 sevenzip.test.mjs
node verify-draft.mjs
```

The tests first authenticate the preserved complete `7z.exe` and `7z.dll` in `../full-archive-baseline-20261012/full-product-x64/`. They invoke the original **only as an independent oracle**. The candidate codec imports only Node‚Äôs standard `zlib` compression primitives. Tests create new private run folders and do not alter retained runs or user data.

| Enforced bound | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Individual file | 16 MiB |
| Total content / solid folder output | 64 MiB |
| Complete archive | 96 MiB |
| Encoded or plain header | 16 MiB |
| Entries, folders, pack streams, total substreams | 10,000 each |
| Name length / path depth | 4,096 UTF-16 code units / 128 components |
| File/archive metadata properties | 100,000 each |
| LZMA2 chunks / declared dictionary | 100,000 / 64 MiB |

UINT64 values use BigInt until checked against their applicable bound. Signature, start-header CRC and next-header CRC are mandatory; available pack, folder and file CRCs are checked before entries are returned. Writer emits pack and folder CRCs plus individual solid-file CRCs. Reader accepts absent optional content CRCs, returning `crc: null` where a file‚Äôs own digest is unavailable; absence is not a verified content check. DEFLATE must consume the entire declared packed stream and produce its exact declared output. LZMA2 stored chunks require the initial dictionary reset, valid controls, complete chunk bodies, exact output and a final terminator. Archive bytes must end at the declared next header, and all preceding packed bytes must belong to supported, non-overlapping streams. Appending another archive or unexplained bytes is rejected.

Names use UTF-16LE with exact terminators. Invalid surrogate pairs, traversal, Windows device names, absolute/drive paths, alternate stream syntax, separators inside components, duplicate/case-colliding paths and file ancestor collisions are rejected. Directory/regular-file metadata must agree with stream presence. Anti-items, links and other special Unix file types are rejected. UTC FILETIME modification dates are read/written at millisecond precision; creation/access dates are validated but not returned or restored. DOS attributes identify file/directory type, but ownership, permissions, ACLs, alternate streams and other attributes are not restored.

**Known gaps:** compressed LZMA and LZMA2 are unavailable; default original 26.04 archives fail explicitly at their LZMA encoded header, and default data with a plain header fails at compressed LZMA2. The manual‚Äôs `cmdline/switches/method.htm:260` and actual original fixtures bind the current default to LZMA2; older `general/7z.htm:41` says LZMA and is not used to override this evidence. Multiple coders, filters/BCJ/BCJ2, complex stream bindings, PPMd, BZip2, Deflate64, encryption/passwords, external/additional metadata streams, nested encoded headers, SFX, split volumes, repair, update operations, streaming writes, multi-worker compression, 100-nanosecond time fidelity, complete GUI/shell behavior and performance parity are not implemented. Known ancillary file properties are bounded/skipped; this does not preserve their semantics. Strict unexplained-byte and trailing-byte rejection excludes some containers the original can tolerate. Everything is synchronous and memory-backed. **Full-feature and all-ways gates remain false.** No complete-product parity claim is made.

The container and method handling are source-assisted: studied pinned official SDK format documents and complete source reader/writer. No original runtime code or third-party codec source is imported into this draft. SDK documents retain their embedded editions (format 18.06, C decoder document 9.35) and are checked against current 26.04 source and executable. The SDK public-domain declaration belongs to the SDK; it does not license the whole original product. Exact source paths, hashes and relevant line references are recorded in the receipt.

Primitive survey documents are pinned by immutable commit and SHA-256 in `primitive-survey/provenance.json`; none are imported. The official SDK is the clearest route to complete raw LZMA/LZMA2 encode/decode, with an explicit source build and bounded adapter. A pure JavaScript port would avoid a new binary but needs a separate correctness/performance investigation. `lzma-purejs` exposes raw decoder/encoder streams and retains MIT notices in its source; its decoder reinitializes state per call, so it is not a drop-in LZMA2 decoder. `LZMA-JS` handles LZMA data and returns text or bytes depending on decoded content; it is not a bounded raw LZMA2 interface. XZ Embedded is a decoding primitive with raw LZMA2 internally, while `xz-decompress` packages an XZ wrapper in WASM and provides no encoder. A new pinned source build/adapter would be required. No C/WASM compiler was found on the current PATH; Node‚Äôs standard zlib supplies no LZMA primitive. These are future choices, not claims of implemented support.

Primary references: [official SDK](https://www.7-zip.org/sdk.html), [complete official source](https://github.com/ip7z/7zip), [XZ Embedded](https://github.com/tukaani-project/xz-embedded), [xz-decompress](https://github.com/httptoolkit/xz-decompress), [LZMA-JS](https://github.com/LZMA-JS/LZMA-JS), [lzma-purejs](https://github.com/cscott/lzma-purejs). The receipt binds the retained exact bytes rather than treating these moving repository links as immutable evidence.
