# Hosted Empty Recycle Bin candidate ‚Äî unexecuted

Only `public-candidate/` is proposed for a later exact public-source copy. It contains an inert source bundle, a new composer, one narrowly extended existing collector and `.github/workflows/vault-empty-bin-cloud.yml`. **No active application/test source is proposed for installation.** The three new reviewable test/helper source files under `candidate/products/rea-studio/apps/vault/` are authoring inputs; their exact bytes appear in the public proposal only as inert `.snapshot` payloads. Do not copy that authoring directory into active apps.

The initial six implementation/test sources remain exactly frozen under `work/vault-empty-bin-next`; its original20 authored groups have not changed. Documentary revisionr2 there separates the safer candidate root-bin refusal from released2.61.1 behavior. Its prior181f92e4 authority/docs remain under `versions/documentation-r1/`.

## Finite source contract

The inert bundle pins exactly nine ephemeral targets: three runtime replacements (`recycle.mjs`, `server.mjs`, `client.mjs`), the initial three test files, a separate adjacent file, the actual original-library test file and its PowerShell helper. Only the first three have current before-images; the other six must be absent from the committed application. All nine after-images and all three before-images are retained; the bundle has13 payloads including its README. The composer authenticates each payload against committed Git before writing any app target, requires clean HEAD matching the hosted commit, copies exact before/after evidence and permits only the exact nine expected visible changes. Partial composition remains a failed receipt.

The fixed **45-file roster** contains every one of the current40 Vault test files plus all five new `.test.mjs` files. The collector rejects missing or additional current Vault tests for this scope. It requires all selected checks to pass with zero skipped/cancelled/failed/todo; the exact total is recorded from actual TAP, without inventing a prior denominator. Twenty original candidate groups, five adjacent groups and two new original-library groups are authored additions, not observed passes. Original operations inside those two groups are separately counted.

The collector receives the same-attempt `vault-empty-bin-composition.json` and existing Flow/Vault prerequisite receipt, independently checks exact KeePass bytes/path and run identity, verifies all nine current/preserved images, then authenticates every other eligible active Studio source to its actual ordinary Git blob. Before/after source inventories, active snapshots and exact visible Git status remain mandatory. No historical development receipt is emitted. `verify-after` is a separate always-attempted9-target source check; a passed collector receipt alone cannot certify the workflow without its matching postcheck and complete evidence.

## Actual library helper and separate adjacent cases

`bin-empty-oracle.ps1` authenticates KeePass2.61.1 executable3,265,880 bytes / `04d29636c393f0c1fc99ff739295494be3adefded93271e44591e8e300225a5a` before assembly loading. It opens an owned bounded synthetic KDBX and calls the actual UUID-designated nonroot bin's `DeleteAllObjects` once. It records named group/settings/entry/history/binary projections before/after, prior/new tombstone order, operation UTC start/end, actual input/output KDBX header versions and optional actual save/reopen. It performs no source-composed deletion, original clock/UUID assignment, GUI or full KeePass program launch. Malformed root-bin fixtures are explicitly excluded from these original comparisons; the candidate's stricter policy stays disclosed.

`bin-empty-original.test.mjs` separately authenticates exact EXE bytes immediately before each child. Each child has a30-second deadline, an owned process-tree kill bounded10seconds and a5-second incomplete-close fallback, plus1MiB per stream. Raw output and JSON running/terminal receipts are synchronized to disk before close/rename. Four planned fixture invocations cover enabled nested/direct contents, disabled existing bin, save/reopen and an already-empty bin. Their exact ID/order, bin/settings and outside named projections must agree with the candidate; actual original deletion timestamps must be within the observed operation bounds. The candidate uses its explicit single clock, and no exact timestamp equality is claimed.

The save fixture deletes the only entry with `QualityCheck=false`, so the released writer may select4.0 after receiving4.1. The helper records the actual header before/after; it does not force4.1 or silently reapply a nondefault outside feature. Named-projection preservation is asserted separately from version identity and arbitrary opaque-XML preservation. The native candidate tests retain their own exact opaque-data/attachment preservation checks.

The collector requires four completed new-library child records, four actual method calls/assembly loads and four successful named comparisons, with zero original GUI/full-program/clock assignments. Existing Vault original comparisons remain additional separately authored tests; this four-call counter covers only the new empty-bin helper. Raw child records and observed version changes remain in the artifact.

The five separate adjacent groups cover disposal during held encrypted persistence, lock serialization, a detached old confirmation while a current group draft is dirty, and render disposal during held preview/committed responses. They supplement the original20 source epoch without editing it. These are DOM-model/in-memory-store checks, not actual browser evidence.

## Hosted execution and evidence

The workflow permits manual dispatch and one narrow push trigger: branch `codex/rea-cloud-recovery-20261015`, limited to its own workflow, experiment-bundle directory and composer path. It has no pull-request trigger or broad application-path trigger. Root explicitly revised the initial manual-only implementation preference to permit the first reviewed branch push to register and launch this workflow. The complete prior17-file manual-only proposal plus its README, plan and tooling pins remain under `versions/manual-only-r1/`. It uses standard GitHub-hosted Windows2022, Node24.16.0, the existing three pinned external actions, read-only contents permission and nonpersisted checkout credentials. It creates no cache or paid runner. The existing Flow/Vault bootstrap authenticates and extracts the complete portable package; its extra Flow npm acquisition is reused but no Flow product tests execute in this scope. The overall job has30minutes, bootstrap8minutes and the test child20minutes.

GitHub's [manual workflow documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/manage-workflow-runs/manually-run-a-workflow) says the workflow must exist on the default branch for first manual triggering; a branch can then be selected. The narrow push trigger resolves the first-run prerequisite without changing the default branch. Manual dispatch remains available after later default-branch integration. A dispatch HTTP204 or queued run does not establish completion.

The evidence directory is an owned `RUNNER_TEMP` child. Streaming raw test/original logs, prerequisite/attempt/composition receipts, active source snapshots, original header/timing observations and independent postcheck are packaged into a<=15MiB portable ZIP, uploaded with one-day retention. Oversize evidence fails the storage gate and may upload only an explicitly incomplete fallback; it cannot certify complete local backup. Runner termination/storage failure can leave incomplete receipts or prevent artifact creation. Actual ZIP/log download, safe extraction, readability/CRC/identity checks and a separate local backup remain required after the hosted run.

No source check, syntax parser, test, application, browser, original library or build has been executed locally. File copying, selected source hashing and source authoring are the only local operations. Root must review and commit the exact public proposal before any hosted execution. No result here establishes installation, full-suite, actual-browser, security, parity, performance or superiority acceptance. Archive work remains separate.

