# Vault Empty Recycle Bin candidate ‚Äî work only

This source-authored candidate fills one explicit, useful workflow gap: permanently empty the current native recycle-bin contents while retaining the bin group, its UUID and settings. The active application currently rejects `emptyRecycleBin`; deleting the bin group through the existing generic group command has different semantics. No active source, old receipt, 159-source registry or 232-capability registry was edited. No candidate, test, syntax checker, browser, original program or build ran locally.

Selection followed bounded reads of the requirements index/crosswalks and all ten application gap records. Those records still require every documented original feature. The old aggregate `KP.ENTRY.001` scope contains historical group-recycle wording; the live Vault README and `server.mjs` explicitly retain the whole-bin gap. This candidate does not claim exhaustive discovery or waive the other gaps. It avoids the current Archive verification/integration work.

## Exact release authority

The official [KeePass integrity page](https://keepass.info/integrity.html), checked on 15 October 2026, lists KeePass2.61.1 source ZIP as 5,333,853 bytes with SHA256 `711bd9ec7076661678469e51bae876debac07a08c32f1b1c6b3b8fe5e761c13a`. The exact selected source is already preserved in the public group-recycle evidence. The official [2.47 release note](https://keepass.info/news/n210109_2.47.html) separately documents the named Empty Recycle Bin menu command; it is documentary context, not a claim that the older release defines2.61.1 behavior.

`SOURCE-CONTRACTS.json` binds2.61.1 `MainForm_Functions.cs:5308‚Äì5344` and `PwGroup.cs:1367‚Äì1386` by complete-file identities and public preservation paths. The handler obtains the bin by UUID, constructs a summary, asks for confirmation, calls `DeleteAllObjects`, then refreshes the UI with the bin retained. The library appends direct active-entry tombstones, recursively deletes child objects, appends each child group's tombstone after its descendants, and clears child collections. It does not append a bin tombstone, traverse entry history as active IDs, or call Touch on the bin or its ancestors.

The existing group source notice and retained complete GPL-2.0-or-later license apply. This extension is source-assisted, not a clean-room/MIT claim. Production code does not call or load the original application. Existing executable/source ZIP carriers stay external and are not recopied into this work candidate.

## Candidate behavior

The candidate directory contains three changed runtime modules and three new authored test files. A complete current application checkout is needed as a future hosted baseline; the candidate directory by itself is an overlay, not a runnable replacement application.

- `recycle.mjs` adds `emptyRecycleBinPreview` and `emptyNativeRecycleBin`. Preview exposes only group names and active entry title/username/URL plus counts. A separate HMAC domain binds the existing complete XML/attachment graph digest, current private key and exact bin UUID. Entry/group command tokens cannot authorize whole-bin deletion. Confirmation must be literal `true`. Tombstone capacity and the operation clock are validated before producing a changed clone. Existing document, depth, XML, entry, group, attachment and tombstone bounds still apply.
- `server.mjs` adds strict `recycleBinPreview` and `emptyRecycleBin` actions. The existing queue captures caller data before waiting. It stages a cloned native document, writes the encrypted backend, and publishes session state only after persistence succeeds and the same session still exists. An already-empty bin produces an explicitly reported no-op without rewriting the encrypted envelope. Missing/dangling bins cannot redirect deletion to a different group. Existing bins may be emptied even when new recycling is disabled, as in the pinned handler.
- `client.mjs` exposes Preview empty recycle bin only on the actual bin row in Show recycled groups. The preview lists counts/names and an unchecked permanent-deletion confirmation. It explicitly says the bin group/settings remain. It reuses the current group-effect exclusion, entry/group draft refusal, active/bin read invalidation, scrub and panel/view generations. Refreshed previews invalidate detached older confirmation controls. Failed persistence requires reopening a fresh unchecked preview; closed/disposed panels cannot publish a held response over a later draft.

The binary attachment table is retained, including newly unreferenced entries, matching the existing conservative preservation policy. Garbage collection is outside this proposal. Unknown bin XML/settings and the outside subtree remain byte-preserved in the normalized document representation. History disappears as part of its deleted entry; history versions receive no independent active-object tombstones.

## Explicit differences and limits

The candidate uses one captured UTC operation time for all new tombstones. Released `DeleteAllObjects` captures UTC separately at each recursive group invocation; a future original comparison must compare ID/order exactly and document timing adaptation or compare actual timestamp bounds. It must not silently overwrite original dates and call the result an unadapted exact comparison.

The UI is a bounded Studio workflow, not a reconstruction of the original summary-dialog layout. It refuses an active/dirty entry editor or group draft and uses explicit in-page confirmation. No checkbox-layout/CSS work is included. No authenticated installer receipt, actual original helper result, native GUI result, real-browser pass, full-suite pass, timing/RAM comparison, security equivalence or superiority is supplied by source authoring.

## Hosted acceptance plan

The three new test files contain **20 authored, unexecuted groups**: nine native-document groups, five encrypted-server/queue groups and six DOM-model groups. These categories are separate from future original-library and actual browser observations.

1. Independently review exact candidate pins, source contracts, target diffs and existing caller fixture identities. Preserve before/after source and all failed attempts. Compose only these six application/test targets in a clean, verified hosted Windows checkout bound to its Git baseline; keep current publication/history verifiers untouched until a separate source-proof transition is reviewed.
2. Run the three authored files together with all existing Vault tests using Node24.16.0, the existing authenticated hosted KeePass prerequisite, zero skips, bounded streams/deadlines, full eligible-source before/after hashes and active snapshots, and a separate postcheck. Acquisition, implementation tests and any originals must have separate counts.
3. Add a narrowly reviewed test-only original-library helper before claiming native parity. Authenticate KeePass.exe `04d29636c393f0c1fc99ff739295494be3adefded93271e44591e8e300225a5a` before each bounded child/assembly load. On owned synthetic KDBX fixtures call the actual `PwGroup.DeleteAllObjects` method on the UUID-designated bin, record start/end UTC, then project bin/settings, outside data, active IDs, prior/new tombstone order and save/reopen behavior. This is one original managed library operation per fixture, zero original GUI/full-program runs. Do not infer original results from source reading or the earlier group-delete campaign.
4. Use an owned random loopback host and data directory for at least four finite real Chromium scenarios: unchecked confirmation/dirty draft refusal, successful nested-bin empty/reload/export, real committed held response after panel close with newer draft, and ordinary persistence rejection with explicit fresh retry. Preserve native PNGs, request observations and before/after source identities. DOM-model tests do not replace these browser cases. Any export comparison must verify downloaded bytes and original reopen separately.
5. Only after the reviewed source experiment actually passes may a separate exact installation/source-proof plan be proposed. Then rerun the full current suite and publication controls against that integrated epoch. All older282/937/1042, browser12, Windows47 and Archive161 receipts stay immutable and are not reattributed or summed.

The active tree remains the separate, parent-owned epoch. This work does not install or commit the candidate. `change-authority.json` records exact current candidate/before/selected-source identities and no execution claim.
